<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Audoleon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned c. 315–285 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Audoleon of Paeonia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Audoleon was king of Paeonia from about 315 to 285 BCE, son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patraos">Patraos</a>. He issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins showing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and a horseman, and with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> he repelled an attack by the Autariatae. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> honoured him with citizenship for gifts of grain, and he married his daughter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus of Epirus</a>. After his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> took control of Paeonia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/audoleon.jpg" alt="Audoleon" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG - Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Audoleon_of_Paeonia_silver_drachm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
